<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01523</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5b9bd6ee-5dc9-4d89-8e28-e1c24e9324c2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down</title>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>The linear function <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is defined by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. </para>
          <para id="para-00002">a. Show that <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> have a
            difference of 4.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item><m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. The difference of
              12 and 8 is 4.</item>
            <item><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">b. Show that when the input value increases from <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, the output values <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> have a
            difference of 4. Use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008">
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, which
            equals 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>The exponential function <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is defined by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            . 
        
    </para>
          <para id="para-00010">a. Show that <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> have a
            quotient of 4. Use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item><m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>64</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. The quotient is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>64</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>,
              which is 4.</item>
            <item><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">b. Show that when the input value increases from <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, the output values <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> have a
            quotient of 4.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00016">
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>,
            which equals 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
        
          <media alt="A hand holds a tablet displaying stacks of gold coins increasing in height, with the text &quot;Power of Compounding&quot; and example calculations showing how $1,000 grows to $1,210 with repeated 10% interest.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_09_01.png" width="500"/>
        </media>
        
        <para id="para-00017">Exponential models can help you understand the power of investment. After
        two years, this investor made $210 off of an initial investment of $1000 because of the 10%
        interest rate.</para>
        <para id="para-00018">Can you calculate how much money the investor
        will have made after twenty years?</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>